248
00:15:36,936 --> 00:15:38,438
BOY: You mean you have
to use your hands?

249
00:15:38,521 --> 00:15:40,690
That's like
a baby's toy.

250
00:15:42,942 --> 00:15:44,527
Baby's toy?

251
00:15:52,785 --> 00:15:54,120
Pepsi Perfect.

252
00:15:54,203 --> 00:15:56,038
Damn!
Pepsi.

253
00:15:58,207 --> 00:15:59,792
GRIFF: Hey, McFly!

254
00:16:01,210 --> 00:16:03,296
I thought I told you
to stay in here!

255
00:16:03,379 --> 00:16:06,757
Griff. Guys,
how's it going?

256
00:16:07,758 --> 00:16:09,427
McFly!
Yeah?

257
00:16:10,386 --> 00:16:11,846
McFly!
What?

258
00:16:11,929 --> 00:16:13,431
Your shoe's unvelked.

259
00:16:14,056 --> 00:16:15,391
(GRIFF LAUGHING)

260
00:16:16,267 --> 00:16:18,311
(LAUGHING SHEEPISHLY)

261
00:16:18,603 --> 00:16:24,233
So, McFly, have you
made a decision about
tonight's opportunity?

262
00:16:24,317 --> 00:16:26,277
Yeah, Griff. You know,
I was thinking about it.
I'm not sure,

263
00:16:26,360 --> 00:16:27,487
because I just think,
you know,

264
00:16:27,570 --> 00:16:30,656
it might be a little
bit dangerous, so...

265
00:16:30,740 --> 00:16:32,033
What's wrong, McFly?

266
00:16:32,116 --> 00:16:33,159
(WHIMPERING)

267
00:16:33,242 --> 00:16:34,994
You got no scrote?

268
00:16:35,077 --> 00:16:36,537
(GROANING)

269
00:16:36,621 --> 00:16:38,414
(ALL LAUGHING)

270
00:16:40,958 --> 00:16:42,168
He's a complete wimp.

271
00:16:42,251 --> 00:16:45,796
What's it gonna be, McFly?
Are you in or out?

272
00:16:46,589 --> 00:16:48,549
I just... I'm not sure
that I should.

273
00:16:48,633 --> 00:16:51,677
You know, because I think
that I should discuss it
with my father.

274
00:16:51,761 --> 00:16:53,304
Your father?
Your father?

275
00:16:53,387 --> 00:16:55,598
Wrong answer, McFly!
You lose!

276
00:16:55,681 --> 00:16:56,933
(SCREAMING)

277
00:16:58,851 --> 00:17:02,021
Okay, Griff, I'll do it.
I'll do it, buddy.
Whatever you say.

278
00:17:02,104 --> 00:17:03,814
Stay down and shut up.

279
00:17:05,274 --> 00:17:06,901
Keep pedaling,
you two!

280
00:17:17,954 --> 00:17:21,958
Now, let's hear
the right answer.

281
00:17:23,459 --> 00:17:24,627
Well!

282
00:17:25,211 --> 00:17:28,130
Since when did you become
the physical type?

283
00:17:28,214 --> 00:17:29,924
The answer's no, Griff.

284
00:17:30,466 --> 00:17:31,509
No?

285
00:17:31,592 --> 00:17:32,718
Yeah. What are you,
deaf and stupid?

286
00:17:32,802 --> 00:17:34,470
I said no!

287
00:17:34,554 --> 00:17:36,806
What's wrong, McFly?
Chicken?

288
00:17:39,642 --> 00:17:41,102
(SYNTHESIZED CLUCKING)

289
00:17:42,645 --> 00:17:44,981
What did you
call me, Griff?

290
00:17:46,148 --> 00:17:48,317
Chicken, McFly!

291
00:17:48,401 --> 00:17:51,112
Nobody calls me

292
00:17:53,906 --> 00:17:55,324
chicken.

293
00:17:55,658 --> 00:17:57,034
(SCREAMING)

294
00:17:57,118 --> 00:17:58,411
(ELECTRICITY SURGING)
(GROANING)

295
00:17:59,829 --> 00:18:01,289
('80S POP MUSIC PLAYING)

296
00:18:02,373 --> 00:18:04,041
(DEVICE WHIRRING)

297
00:18:05,418 --> 00:18:07,336
All right, punk!
Hey, look!

298
00:18:14,844 --> 00:18:16,220
(ALL GROANING)

299
00:18:22,935 --> 00:18:25,271
Hey, hey, hey, hey, hey.
Hey, hey, hey.

300
00:18:25,354 --> 00:18:27,773
Stop! Little girl,
little girl. Stop.

301
00:18:27,857 --> 00:18:28,941
Hey.
Look,

302
00:18:29,025 --> 00:18:31,777
I need to borrow
your Hoverboard.

303
00:18:32,278 --> 00:18:33,571
Where is he?

304
00:18:33,654 --> 00:18:34,780
Here.

305
00:18:35,531 --> 00:18:36,907
There!

306
00:18:43,039 --> 00:18:44,999
He's on a Hoverboard.

307
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:48,711
Get the boards!

308
00:18:48,794 --> 00:18:50,379
GIRL: Get McFly!

309
00:18:50,463 --> 00:18:51,964
(SCREAMING)

310
00:18:57,303 --> 00:18:58,471
Get him!

311
00:19:07,229 --> 00:19:09,231
Yeah!
Yeah, we got him!

312
00:19:13,944 --> 00:19:15,154
(TIRES SCREECHING)

313
00:19:20,159 --> 00:19:22,620
There's something
very familiar
about all this.

314
00:19:23,746 --> 00:19:25,164
(GRUNTING)

315
00:19:55,820 --> 00:19:57,905
Hey, McFly, you bojo!

316
00:19:57,988 --> 00:19:59,865
Those boards
don't work on water!

317
00:19:59,949 --> 00:20:01,951
Unless you've
got power!

318
00:20:02,034 --> 00:20:03,285
(LAUGHING)

319
00:20:03,953 --> 00:20:05,287
(PANTING)

320
00:20:27,643 --> 00:20:28,936
Hook on!

321
00:20:37,945 --> 00:20:39,363
Batter up!

322
00:20:56,505 --> 00:20:58,299
(SCREAMING)

323
00:21:08,726 --> 00:21:10,227
Holy shit!

324
00:21:13,814 --> 00:21:15,316
(PEOPLE CLAMORING)

325
00:21:15,399 --> 00:21:16,901
Buttheads.

326
00:21:19,653 --> 00:21:21,655
(SIREN WAILING)

327
00:21:24,950 --> 00:21:26,494
(ELECTRONIC BEEPING)

328
00:21:29,997 --> 00:21:32,082
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Drying mode on.</i>

329
00:21:32,166 --> 00:21:33,709
<i>Jacket drying.</i>

330
00:21:34,585 --> 00:21:36,045
(CONFIRMATION BEEP)

331
00:21:36,212 --> 00:21:38,214
<i>Your jacket is now dry.</i>

332
00:21:41,717 --> 00:21:44,136
Hey, kid.
Hey, little girl, thanks.

333
00:21:44,220 --> 00:21:46,722
Keep it.
I got a Pit Bull now.

334
00:21:46,806 --> 00:21:47,932
Come on. Let's go.

335
00:21:48,015 --> 00:21:49,308
MAN: Save the clock tower!

336
00:21:49,391 --> 00:21:51,560
Hey, kid.
Throw in 100 bucks, will you,

337
00:21:51,644 --> 00:21:53,729
and help save
the clock tower.

338
00:21:53,813 --> 00:21:55,231
Sorry, no.
Come on, kid.

339
00:21:55,314 --> 00:21:56,816
That's an important
historical landmark!

340
00:21:56,899 --> 00:21:58,108
Look, some other time.

341
00:21:58,192 --> 00:22:00,903
MAN: Lightning struck
that thing 60 years ago.

342
00:22:00,986 --> 00:22:04,114
MARTY: Wait a minute.
Cubs win World Series.

343
00:22:06,116 --> 00:22:07,159
Against Miami?

344
00:22:07,243 --> 00:22:08,661
Yeah, it's something, huh?

345
00:22:08,744 --> 00:22:11,288
Who would've thought?
100-1 shot!

346
00:22:11,372 --> 00:22:13,958
I wish I could go back
to the beginning
of the season,

347
00:22:14,041 --> 00:22:15,251
put some money
on the Cubs.

348
00:22:15,334 --> 00:22:17,545
I just meant
that Miami...

349
00:22:18,587 --> 00:22:19,880
What did you just say?

350
00:22:19,964 --> 00:22:22,383
I said I wish I could
go back to the beginning
of the season.

351
00:22:22,466 --> 00:22:24,802
Put some money
on the Cubbies!

352
00:22:29,056 --> 00:22:32,142
Now this has
an interesting feature.
It has a dust jacket.

353
00:22:32,226 --> 00:22:34,520
Books used to have these
to protect the covers.

354
00:22:34,603 --> 00:22:36,188
Of course,
that was before they had
dust-repellent paper.

355
00:22:36,272 --> 00:22:37,273
Ah.

356
00:22:37,356 --> 00:22:38,983
And if you're
interested in dust,

357
00:22:39,066 --> 00:22:41,944
we have a quaint
little piece
from the 1980s.

358
00:22:42,027 --> 00:22:43,112
It's called a DustBuster.

359
00:22:47,658 --> 00:22:50,244
I can't lose.
Marty! Marty! Up here!

360
00:22:50,327 --> 00:22:51,370
(WHISTLES)

361
00:22:51,453 --> 00:22:52,746
Hey, Doc,
what's going on?

362
00:22:52,830 --> 00:22:54,623
Stand by.
I'll park over there.

363
00:22:55,332 --> 00:22:57,001
MARTY: Yeah, all right.

364
00:22:58,961 --> 00:23:00,838
Hey, right on time.

365
00:23:00,921 --> 00:23:02,506
Flying DeLorean.

366
00:23:02,590 --> 00:23:05,009
I haven't seen one
of those in 30 years.

367
00:23:05,092 --> 00:23:06,093
(BOTH GRUNTING)

368
00:23:06,176 --> 00:23:07,344
Sorry. Excuse me.
Sorry.

369
00:23:07,428 --> 00:23:08,888
(HORN HONKING)

370
00:23:08,971 --> 00:23:11,807
Hey, I'm walking here!
I'm walking here!

371
00:23:12,725 --> 00:23:13,726
What the hell?

372
00:23:13,809 --> 00:23:17,146
Don't drive, trank,
low-res scuzzball!

373
00:23:18,188 --> 00:23:19,523
Two of them?

374
00:23:20,024 --> 00:23:22,234
I left him in a suspended
animation kennel.

375
00:23:22,318 --> 00:23:23,736
Einstein never
knew I was gone!

376
00:23:23,819 --> 00:23:24,862
(SIREN WAILING)

377
00:23:24,945 --> 00:23:29,116
Marty! What in the name
of Sir Isaac H. Newton
happened here?

378
00:23:29,575 --> 00:23:32,953
Oh, yeah, Doc, listen,
my kid showed up.
All hell broke loose.

379
00:23:33,037 --> 00:23:34,580
Your kid?

380
00:23:34,663 --> 00:23:38,125
Great Scott,
the sleep inducer.
I was afraid of this.

381
00:23:38,208 --> 00:23:39,418
Because I used it
on Jennifer,

382
00:23:39,501 --> 00:23:40,502
there wasn't
enough power left

383
00:23:40,586 --> 00:23:43,088
to knock your son out
for a full hour. Damn!

384
00:23:43,172 --> 00:23:45,424
Doc, Doc, Doc,
look at this!

385
00:23:45,507 --> 00:23:47,176
It's changing.

386
00:23:53,015 --> 00:23:54,934
(INDISTINCT YELLING)

387
00:23:59,688 --> 00:24:01,941
I was framed!

388
00:24:02,024 --> 00:24:05,069
Yes! Yes, of course!

389
00:24:06,236 --> 00:24:08,489
Because this
Hoverboard incident
has now occurred,

390
00:24:08,572 --> 00:24:10,449
Griff now goes to jail.

391
00:24:10,532 --> 00:24:12,034
Therefore,
your son won't go
with him tonight,

392
00:24:12,117 --> 00:24:13,994
and that robbery
will never take place.

393
00:24:14,078 --> 00:24:16,956
Thus, history,
future history,

394
00:24:17,039 --> 00:24:19,416
has now been altered,
and this is the proof!

395
00:24:19,500 --> 00:24:21,835
Marty, we've succeeded,
not exactly as I planned,
but no matter.

396
00:24:21,919 --> 00:24:24,046
Let's go get Jennifer
and go home!

397
00:24:26,465 --> 00:24:28,384
Hi, Einie. Hi, buddy.
(WHIMPERING)

398
00:24:30,010 --> 00:24:31,136
What's this?

399
00:24:31,220 --> 00:24:32,346
It's a souvenir.

400
00:24:32,429 --> 00:24:34,723
"50 years of
sports statistics."

401
00:24:34,807 --> 00:24:37,059
Hardly recreational
reading material, Marty.

402
00:24:37,142 --> 00:24:38,143
Well, hey, Doc,
what's the harm

403
00:24:38,227 --> 00:24:40,437
in bringing back
a little info
on the future?

404
00:24:40,521 --> 00:24:42,564
You know, maybe we could
place a couple bets.

405
00:24:42,648 --> 00:24:46,652
Marty, I didn't
invent the time machine
for financial gain!

406
00:24:46,735 --> 00:24:49,446
The intent here is
to gain a clearer
perception of humanity.

407
00:24:49,530 --> 00:24:50,948
Where we've been,
where we're going,

408
00:24:51,031 --> 00:24:54,201
the pitfalls and
the possibilities,
the perils and the promise.

409
00:24:54,284 --> 00:24:57,579
Perhaps even an answer
to that universal question,
"Why?"

410
00:24:57,663 --> 00:24:59,832
Hey, Doc,
I'm all for that.

411
00:24:59,915 --> 00:25:02,418
What's wrong with making
a few bucks on the side?

412
00:25:02,501 --> 00:25:06,005
I am going to put
this in the trash.

413
00:25:06,088 --> 00:25:07,840
(CHATTERING ON POLICE RADIO)

414
00:25:07,923 --> 00:25:09,299
Great Scott!

415
00:25:12,761 --> 00:25:17,016
McFly,
Jennifer Jane Parker,
3793 Oakhurst Street,

416
00:25:17,099 --> 00:25:18,517
Hilldale, age 47.

417
00:25:18,726 --> 00:25:21,311
Forty-seven?
That's a hell of
a good face-lift.

418
00:25:21,729 --> 00:25:22,896
What the hell are
they doing, Doc?

419
00:25:22,980 --> 00:25:24,940
They used her thumbprint
to assess her ID.

420
00:25:25,024 --> 00:25:26,650
Since her thumbprint
never changes
over the years,

421
00:25:26,734 --> 00:25:28,861
they simply assume
she's the Jennifer
of the future.

422
00:25:28,944 --> 00:25:30,154
Well, we gotta stop them.

423
00:25:30,237 --> 00:25:31,905
What are we gonna say?
That we're time travelers?

424
00:25:31,989 --> 00:25:33,240
They'd have us committed.

425
00:25:33,323 --> 00:25:35,701
She's clean.
That means
we take her home.

426
00:25:35,784 --> 00:25:37,036
Home? To Hilldale?

427
00:25:37,119 --> 00:25:39,079
It'll be dark
by the time
we get out there.

428
00:25:39,163 --> 00:25:42,541
That's it.
They're taking her home
to your future home!

429
00:25:42,624 --> 00:25:43,834
We'll arrive
shortly thereafter,

430
00:25:43,917 --> 00:25:45,919
get her out of there
and go back to 1985.

431
00:25:46,003 --> 00:25:47,254
You mean I'm gonna
see where I live?

432
00:25:47,337 --> 00:25:48,881
I'm gonna see myself
as an old man?

433
00:25:48,964 --> 00:25:52,092
No, no, no, Marty.
That could result in...
(GASPS) Great Scott!

434
00:25:52,551 --> 00:25:55,304
Jennifer could
conceivably encounter
her future self!

435
00:25:55,387 --> 00:25:58,432
The consequences of that
could be disastrous!

436
00:25:58,515 --> 00:25:59,725
Doc, what do you mean?

437
00:25:59,808 --> 00:26:01,268
I foresee
two possibilities.

438
00:26:01,351 --> 00:26:03,854
One, coming face to face
with herself 30 years older

439
00:26:03,937 --> 00:26:06,065
would put her into shock,
and she'd simply pass out,

440
00:26:06,148 --> 00:26:08,817
or two,
the encounter could
create a time paradox,

441
00:26:08,901 --> 00:26:11,570
the results of which could
cause a chain reaction
that would unravel

442
00:26:11,653 --> 00:26:13,322
the very fabric of
the space-time continuum

443
00:26:13,405 --> 00:26:15,657
and destroy
the entire universe!

444
00:26:15,741 --> 00:26:17,451
Granted, that's
a worst-case scenario.

445
00:26:17,534 --> 00:26:19,161
The destruction might,
in fact, be very localized,

446
00:26:19,244 --> 00:26:21,497
limited to
merely our own galaxy.

447
00:26:22,372 --> 00:26:24,249
Well, that's a relief.

448
00:26:28,170 --> 00:26:31,840
Let's go. I sure hope
we find Jennifer
before she finds herself.

449
00:26:33,467 --> 00:26:36,637
The skyway's jammed.
It's gonna take us
forever to get there.

450
00:26:36,720 --> 00:26:38,222
And this stays here.

451
00:26:38,305 --> 00:26:40,307
I didn't invent
the time machine
to win at gambling.

452
00:26:40,390 --> 00:26:42,768
I invented a time machine
to travel through time!

453
00:26:42,851 --> 00:26:44,853
I know. I know.
I know, Doc.

454
00:26:54,071 --> 00:26:58,534
So, Doc Brown
invented a time machine.

455
00:27:24,893 --> 00:27:26,395
Hilldale.

456
00:27:27,396 --> 00:27:31,233
Nothing but a breeding
ground for tranks,
lobos and zipheads.

457
00:27:31,316 --> 00:27:33,193
Yeah, they ought to tear
this whole place down.

458
00:27:34,570 --> 00:27:35,571
(BEEPS)

459
00:27:35,654 --> 00:27:36,780
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Welcome home, Jennifer.</i>

460
00:27:36,864 --> 00:27:37,990
Whoa!

461
00:27:38,657 --> 00:27:40,033
You all right?
Yeah.

462
00:27:40,117 --> 00:27:42,870
You got a little tranked,
but I think you can walk.

463
00:27:42,953 --> 00:27:44,496
Ma'am,
you should reprogram.

464
00:27:44,580 --> 00:27:46,498
It's dangerous to
enter without lights on.

465
00:27:46,582 --> 00:27:47,541
JENNIFER: Lights on?

466
00:27:47,624 --> 00:27:48,667
Yes. Now, look.

467
00:27:49,793 --> 00:27:52,337
Just take it easy
and you'll be fine.

468
00:27:52,421 --> 00:27:54,089
And be careful
in the future.

469
00:27:55,090 --> 00:27:56,592
The future?

470
00:27:57,885 --> 00:27:59,928
Have a nice day,
Mrs. McFly.

471
00:28:02,681 --> 00:28:06,101
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Broadcasting beautiful views
24 hours a day,</i>

472
00:28:06,185 --> 00:28:08,437
