Grandma Lorraine!

482
00:28:58,612 --> 00:28:59,571
Sweetheart!

483
00:28:59,655 --> 00:29:00,822
Hi!
Hi!

484
00:29:00,906 --> 00:29:01,907
What happened
to Grandpa?

485
00:29:01,990 --> 00:29:03,742
Oh, he threw
his back out again.

486
00:29:03,825 --> 00:29:05,786
How's Grandad's
little pumpkin?

487
00:29:05,869 --> 00:29:07,246
How did you do that?
How did he do that?

488
00:29:07,329 --> 00:29:09,748
Oh, out on the
golf course. (GROANS)

489
00:29:09,831 --> 00:29:10,916
Are your folks home yet?

490
00:29:10,999 --> 00:29:12,668
I brought pizza
for everyone.

491
00:29:12,751 --> 00:29:13,752
Oh, who's going
to eat all that?

492
00:29:13,835 --> 00:29:14,836
Oh, I will.

493
00:29:15,087 --> 00:29:16,922
Damn this traffic!

494
00:29:17,005 --> 00:29:18,924
Jennifer,
that is Old Jennifer,
usually gets home around now.

495
00:29:19,007 --> 00:29:20,550
I hope we're
not too late.

496
00:29:24,513 --> 00:29:25,764
What is it?
What's the matter, Doc?

497
00:29:25,847 --> 00:29:28,433
For a moment,
I thought I saw a taxi
in my rear display.

498
00:29:28,517 --> 00:29:30,686
I thought it was
following us. Weird.

499
00:29:31,186 --> 00:29:33,939
OLDEST LORRAINE:
I can't believe this
window's still broken.

500
00:29:34,022 --> 00:29:36,775
MARLENE: Well, when
the scenescreen repairman
called Daddy a chicken,

501
00:29:36,858 --> 00:29:38,443
Daddy threw him
out of the house.

502
00:29:38,527 --> 00:29:40,529
Now, we can't get
anybody to fix it.

503
00:29:40,612 --> 00:29:42,990
Oh, look how
worn out this is.

504
00:29:43,991 --> 00:29:46,451
Your father's
biggest problem, Marlene,

505
00:29:46,535 --> 00:29:48,453
is that he loses
all self-control

506
00:29:48,537 --> 00:29:50,414
when someone
calls him chicken.

507
00:29:50,497 --> 00:29:52,291
How many times have
we heard it, George?

508
00:29:52,374 --> 00:29:54,209
"Mom, I can't let them
think I'm chicken."

509
00:29:54,293 --> 00:29:55,961
"Can't let them
think I'm chicken."

510
00:29:56,044 --> 00:29:58,297
You're right.
Well, you're right!

511
00:29:58,380 --> 00:29:59,798
OLDEST LORRAINE:
About 30 years ago,

512
00:29:59,881 --> 00:30:01,925
your father tried to
prove he wasn't chicken,

513
00:30:02,009 --> 00:30:04,177
and he ended up in
an automobile accident.

514
00:30:04,261 --> 00:30:05,595
MARLENE: Oh, you mean with
the Rolls-Royce?

515
00:30:05,971 --> 00:30:08,056
Automobile accident.

516
00:30:19,776 --> 00:30:22,404
All right, Einie,
let's find Jennifer.

517
00:30:23,322 --> 00:30:24,865
I don't believe it.
I live in Hilldale?

518
00:30:24,948 --> 00:30:26,491
This is great!
Way to go, McFly.

519
00:30:26,575 --> 00:30:28,493
Marty, stay here.
Just change clothes.

520
00:30:28,577 --> 00:30:29,578
If I need you,
I'll holler.

521
00:30:29,661 --> 00:30:31,330
Come on, Doc, I wanna
check out my house.

522
00:30:31,413 --> 00:30:33,332
We can't risk you running
into your older self.

523
00:30:33,415 --> 00:30:35,250
Come on, Einie,
let's go.

524
00:30:35,334 --> 00:30:37,753
Where's Jennifer?
Where's Jennifer?

525
00:30:37,836 --> 00:30:39,421
Hilldale.

526
00:30:40,464 --> 00:30:41,590
This is bitching.

527
00:30:47,679 --> 00:30:50,390
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>One, seven,
four point five zero.</i>

528
00:30:50,474 --> 00:30:51,850
That will be $174.50.

529
00:30:51,933 --> 00:30:53,101
Here.

530
00:30:53,185 --> 00:30:54,936
I'd be careful, old timer.
This is a rough neighborhood.

531
00:30:55,020 --> 00:30:56,813
Where's my receipt?
Right here. Here it is.

532
00:30:56,897 --> 00:30:59,149
PARROT: Hello. Hello.
How about a tip?

533
00:30:59,232 --> 00:31:00,901
(PARROT LAUGHING)

534
00:31:13,663 --> 00:31:15,624
That accident caused
a chain reaction

535
00:31:15,707 --> 00:31:17,793
that sent Marty's life
straight down the tubes.

536
00:31:17,876 --> 00:31:19,878
If not for that accident,
your father's life

537
00:31:19,961 --> 00:31:21,713
would have turned out
very differently.

538
00:31:21,797 --> 00:31:23,882
The man in the Rolls-Royce
wouldn't have
pressed charges,

539
00:31:23,965 --> 00:31:25,133
Marty wouldn't
have broken his hand,

540
00:31:25,217 --> 00:31:26,968
and he wouldn't have
given up on his music,

541
00:31:27,052 --> 00:31:29,971
and he wouldn't have
spent all those years
feeling sorry for himself.

542
00:31:30,055 --> 00:31:31,640
Hey, Mom, nice pants.

543
00:31:31,723 --> 00:31:34,726
I think the real reason
your mother married him
was because

544
00:31:34,810 --> 00:31:36,478
she felt sorry for him.
"Mom"?

545
00:31:37,104 --> 00:31:38,397
Such a sweet girl.

546
00:31:38,480 --> 00:31:40,399
Art off.
OLDEST LORRAINE: I think
maybe she deserves...

547
00:31:40,482 --> 00:31:45,737
Okay, I want channels
18, 24, 63, 109, 87
and the Weather Channel.

548
00:31:45,821 --> 00:31:47,406
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>The Weather Channel,
bringing you</i>

549
00:31:47,489 --> 00:31:48,740
<i>the world's weather
24 hours a day.</i>

550
00:31:48,824 --> 00:31:49,950
(CHATTERING ON TV)

551
00:31:50,033 --> 00:31:51,076
<i>Weather conditions
remain the same...</i>

552
00:31:51,159 --> 00:31:52,828
<i>All board-certified
implant surgeons...</i>

553
00:31:52,911 --> 00:31:54,413
<i>With a minor warm front...</i>

554
00:31:54,496 --> 00:31:56,081
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Welcome home, Marty.</i>

555
00:31:56,164 --> 00:31:58,166
MARTY JR.: Hey, hey, hey.
Dad's home.

556
00:31:58,583 --> 00:32:01,128
That's right.
He's home.
Dad's home.

557
00:32:01,253 --> 00:32:03,171
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Lord of the manor.</i>
Hello. Hello.

558
00:32:03,255 --> 00:32:04,297
<i>King of the castle.</i>

559
00:32:04,381 --> 00:32:05,424
Hello.

560
00:32:07,050 --> 00:32:08,927
What the hell is this?

561
00:32:09,010 --> 00:32:11,138
(BEEPS)
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Lithium mode on.</i>

562
00:32:11,221 --> 00:32:13,974
Yeah. That's better.
Damned kids.

563
00:32:14,057 --> 00:32:15,976
AUTOMATED VOICE: <i>The best...</i>
Hey, Son.

564
00:32:17,561 --> 00:32:19,521
Watching a little TV
for a change?

565
00:32:29,823 --> 00:32:31,366
(BEEPING)

566
00:32:32,075 --> 00:32:33,535
Son of a...

567
00:32:53,430 --> 00:32:55,056
(ENGINE STARTING)

568
00:33:13,992 --> 00:33:15,285
MARTY JR.: Hey, pizza.
I'm hungry.

569
00:33:15,368 --> 00:33:16,536
OLDER MARTY: All right.
Just wait your turn.

570
00:33:16,620 --> 00:33:17,662
MARTY JR.: Grandma,
when it's ready,

571
00:33:17,746 --> 00:33:19,414
could you just
shove it in my mouth?
(LAUGHING HUMOROUSLY)

572
00:33:19,498 --> 00:33:22,042
OLDER MARTY:
Don't you be a smartass.

573
00:33:22,125 --> 00:33:24,002
MARTY JR.: Oh, great!
The Atrocity Channel.

574
00:33:24,085 --> 00:33:25,754
Hydrate level 4, please.

575
00:33:25,837 --> 00:33:27,172
(GURGLING)

576
00:33:27,255 --> 00:33:28,673
(BELL DINGS)

577
00:33:30,592 --> 00:33:31,927
Mmm.

578
00:33:33,136 --> 00:33:34,387
MARTY JR.: Is it ready?

579
00:33:34,471 --> 00:33:36,014
OLDEST LORRAINE:
Here you go.

580
00:33:36,097 --> 00:33:37,182
OLDER MARTY:
Oh, boy. Oh, boy.

581
00:33:37,265 --> 00:33:40,060
Mom, you sure
can hydrate a pizza.

582
00:33:40,143 --> 00:33:42,270
I'm sorry. I missed
that whole thing.

583
00:33:42,354 --> 00:33:46,483
OLDEST LORRAINE: Well, I'm
just worried about Jennifer.
Why isn't she home yet?

584
00:33:46,566 --> 00:33:50,111
I'm not sure
where Jennifer is, Mom.

585
00:33:50,195 --> 00:33:52,197
She should have
been home hours ago.

586
00:33:52,280 --> 00:33:53,865
I'm having a hard time
keeping track of
her these days.

587
00:33:53,949 --> 00:33:56,660
Hey, fruit.
Fruit, please. Thank you.

588
00:33:56,743 --> 00:33:58,745
She's in one of
those moods, I guess.
I don't know.

589
00:33:58,828 --> 00:34:00,497
OLDEST LORRAINE:
Aren't you and Jennifer
getting along?

590
00:34:00,580 --> 00:34:01,831
Oh, yeah.
Great, Mom.

591
00:34:01,915 --> 00:34:04,209
We're more like
a couple of teenagers,
you know.

592
00:34:04,292 --> 00:34:05,585
(PHONE RINGING)

593
00:34:06,711 --> 00:34:08,505
Dad, telephone.
It's Needles.

594
00:34:09,297 --> 00:34:10,715
Dad, it's for you.

595
00:34:11,800 --> 00:34:14,719
All right. Well,
I'll take that in the den.
Excuse me.

596
00:34:14,803 --> 00:34:16,680
Retract.

597
00:34:16,763 --> 00:34:19,808
Hello.
I'm in here, please.

598
00:34:20,475 --> 00:34:24,813
<i>Hey, the big M.
How's it hanging, McFly?</i>

599
00:34:25,522 --> 00:34:26,648
Hey, Needles.

600
00:34:26,731 --> 00:34:27,857
Needles?

601
00:34:27,941 --> 00:34:30,318
<i>So, did you take look
at that little business
proposal of mine?</i>

602
00:34:30,402 --> 00:34:31,611
OLDER MARTY:
I don't know, Needles.

603
00:34:31,695 --> 00:34:33,238
NEEDLES: <i>What are
you afraid of?</i>

604
00:34:33,321 --> 00:34:36,908
<i>If this thing works,
it'll solve all your
financial problems.</i>

605
00:34:36,992 --> 00:34:40,120
And if it
doesn't work, Needles,
I could get fired.

606
00:34:40,203 --> 00:34:44,040
It's illegal.
I mean, what if the Jits
is monitoring, huh?

607
00:34:44,124 --> 00:34:46,251
<i>The Jits will
never find out.</i>

608
00:34:46,334 --> 00:34:47,419
Oh, God.

609
00:34:47,502 --> 00:34:52,090
<i>Come on. Stick your
card in the slot,
and I'll handle it.</i>

610
00:34:52,757 --> 00:34:56,845
<i>Unless you want
everyone in the division
to think you're chicken.</i>

611
00:35:01,391 --> 00:35:04,644
Nobody calls me chicken,
Needles.

612
00:35:04,728 --> 00:35:05,729
Nobody!

613
00:35:05,812 --> 00:35:06,813
NEEDLES: <i>All right.</i>

614
00:35:07,814 --> 00:35:09,024
<i>Prove it.</i>

615
00:35:13,570 --> 00:35:15,071
All right.

616
00:35:15,572 --> 00:35:17,365
All right, Needles.

617
00:35:19,075 --> 00:35:20,410
(SCOFFS)

618
00:35:20,744 --> 00:35:24,039
Here's my card.
Scan it. I'm in.

619
00:35:24,581 --> 00:35:27,542
<i>Thanks, McFly.
I'll see you at
the plant tomorrow.</i>

620
00:35:29,377 --> 00:35:30,545
(AUTOMATED VOICE READING)

621
00:35:30,629 --> 00:35:31,963
Chicken.

622
00:35:33,715 --> 00:35:35,091
<i>McFly!</i>

623
00:35:35,884 --> 00:35:38,470
(GREETING IN JAPANESE)

624
00:35:38,553 --> 00:35:43,058
<i>McFly, I was monitoring
that scan you
just interfaced.</i>

625
00:35:43,141 --> 00:35:44,809
<i>You are terminated!</i>

626
00:35:44,893 --> 00:35:46,144
Terminated. No!

627
00:35:46,227 --> 00:35:48,396
No! It wasn't
my fault, sir.
It was Needles.

628
00:35:48,480 --> 00:35:49,522
Needles was
behind the whole thing.

629
00:35:49,606 --> 00:35:50,607
<i>And you cooperated.</i>

630
00:35:50,732 --> 00:35:52,859
No, I didn't.
It was a sting operation.
<i>It was illegal.</i>

631
00:35:52,942 --> 00:35:54,235
I was setting him up.

632
00:35:54,319 --> 00:35:55,987
<i>And you knew.
McFly, read my fax!</i>

633
00:35:56,071 --> 00:36:00,200
No! Please, no.
I cannot be fired.
I'm fired.

634
00:36:00,283 --> 00:36:02,118
(EXCLAIMS IN DISAPPOINTMENT)

635
00:36:02,243 --> 00:36:04,537
(WHIRRING)

636
00:36:11,419 --> 00:36:13,922
Oh, this is heavy.

637
00:36:14,756 --> 00:36:17,342
What am I going
to tell Jennifer?

638
00:36:17,425 --> 00:36:19,094
Jennifer. Jennifer.

639
00:36:19,427 --> 00:36:21,763
Oh, Doc, am I glad
to see you.

640
00:36:22,263 --> 00:36:25,350
Go out the front door.
I'll meet you there.

641
00:36:25,433 --> 00:36:27,936
But it doesn't open.
There's no doorknob.

642
00:36:28,019 --> 00:36:30,230
Press your thumb
to the plate.

643
00:36:30,313 --> 00:36:32,190
What plate?

644
00:36:45,120 --> 00:36:46,204
(DOOR OPENS)

645
00:36:46,287 --> 00:36:48,331
Marty, what does
this fax mean?

646
00:36:48,415 --> 00:36:51,543
Oh, Mom, it's a joke,
an office joke.
Kind of a joke fax.

647
00:36:51,626 --> 00:36:52,877
OLDEST LORRAINE:
Marty, I heard you yelling.

648
00:36:52,961 --> 00:36:54,754
Mom, Mom, Mom,
calm down.

649
00:36:54,838 --> 00:36:57,132
I wasn't yelling.
Needles and I were
just kind of joking.

650
00:36:57,215 --> 00:36:58,341
AUTOMATED VOICE:
<i>Welcome home, Jennifer.</i>

651
00:36:58,425 --> 00:36:59,426
OLDEST LORRAINE: Marty,
have you lost your job?

652
00:36:59,509 --> 00:37:02,011
Lost my job, Mom?
Get out of town.
Look, you know...

653
00:37:05,473 --> 00:37:06,766
(BOTH GASPING)

654
00:37:06,850 --> 00:37:08,893
I'm young!
I'm old!

655
00:37:18,153 --> 00:37:19,738
Marty! Marty!

656
00:37:19,821 --> 00:37:21,865
Marty, come quick!
Quick!

657
00:37:27,954 --> 00:37:29,497
(GRUNTING)

658
00:37:29,873 --> 00:37:31,166
(GROANING)

659
00:37:37,505 --> 00:37:38,840
She encountered
her older self

660
00:37:38,923 --> 00:37:41,468
and went into shock,
just as I predicted.

661
00:37:41,551 --> 00:37:43,011
She'll be fine.

662
00:37:43,094 --> 00:37:45,263
Let's get her back
to 1985,

663
00:37:45,346 --> 00:37:48,141
and then I'm gonna destroy
the time machine.

664
00:37:48,224 --> 00:37:49,309
Destroy it?

665
00:37:49,392 --> 00:37:51,102
What about that
stuff about humanity,

666
00:37:51,186 --> 00:37:52,812
where we're going
and why?

667
00:37:52,896 --> 00:37:55,440
The risks
are just too great,
as this incident proves.

668
00:37:55,523 --> 00:37:56,775
And I was
behaving responsibly.

669
00:37:56,858 --> 00:37:58,151
You can imagine
the danger

670
00:37:58,234 --> 00:38:00,278
if the time machine
were to fall
into the wrong hands.

671
00:38:00,904 --> 00:38:02,530
(GROANING)

672
00:38:03,531 --> 00:38:05,867
My only regret is that
I'll never get a chance

673
00:38:05,950 --> 00:38:08,411
to visit my favorite
historical era,

674
00:38:08,620 --> 00:38:10,038
the Old West.

675
00:38:10,413 --> 00:38:14,167
But time traveling
is just too dangerous.

676
00:38:14,250 --> 00:38:17,170
Better that I devote
myself to studying

677
00:38:17,253 --> 00:38:20,173
the other great mystery
of the universe,

678
00:38:22,509 --> 00:38:23,718
women.

679
00:38:25,720 --> 00:38:29,390
Marty, Einie,
brace yourselves for
temporal displacement.

680
00:38:34,562 --> 00:38:36,397
(ENGINE HUMMING)

681
00:38:39,776 --> 00:38:41,402
Did we make it?

682
00:38:45,323 --> 00:38:46,699
Are we back?

683
00:38:48,076 --> 00:38:49,577
(SCREAMING)

684
00:38:53,540 --> 00:38:54,582
We're back.

685
00:39:01,840 --> 00:39:03,466
(TIRES SCREECHING)

686
00:39:15,478 --> 00:39:16,855
Let's put her
in the swing.

687
00:39:16,938 --> 00:39:18,231
Then I'll take you home,
and you can come back

688
00:39:18,314 --> 00:39:20,233
in your truck
and wake her.

689
00:39:20,316 --> 00:39:22,777
When she awakens here
in her own house
and it's dark,

690
00:39:22,861 --> 00:39:25,613
you should be
able to convince her
that it was all a dream.

691
00:39:25,697 --> 00:39:26,865
Wait a minute.
We're just gonna

692
00:39:26,948 --> 00:39:28,449
leave her here
on the porch?

693
00:39:28,533 --> 00:39:31,786
The disorientation
will help convince her
that it was all a dream.

694
00:39:31,870 --> 00:39:33,746
How long do you think
she's gonna be out?

695
00:39:33,830 --> 00:39:36,374
I'm not quite sure.
She received quite
a shock.

696
00:39:36,457 --> 00:39:37,667
Could be for
a few minutes.

697
00:39:37,750 --> 00:39:40,086
Most probably,
a couple of hours.

698
00:39:40,169 --> 00:39:41,880
You better
bring smelling salts
back with you.

699
00:39:41,963 --> 00:39:43,256
You're the doc, Doc.

700
00:39:43,339 --> 00:39:44,382
All right. Come on.
Let's go, Einie.

701
00:39:44,465 --> 00:39:45,925
(EINSTEIN WHIMPERING)

702
00:39:47,176 --> 00:39:49,220
Don't worry.
She'll be fine.

703
00:39:50,305 --> 00:39:53,016
I don't remember bars
being on these windows.

704
00:39:59,856 --> 00:40:01,482
(DOGS GROWLING)

705
00:40:09,699 --> 00:40:12,785
If you need me,
I'll be back at my lab
dismantling this thing.

706
00:40:12,869 --> 00:40:14,078
Right.

707
00:40:31,429 --> 00:40:33,097
What the hell?

708
00:40:49,238 --> 00:40:50,865
(SCREAMING)
(GASPS)

709
00:40:50,949 --> 00:40:52,659
Hey. Hey, wait.
Wait a minute.

710
00:40:52,742 --> 00:40:54,452
(STUTTERING) What are
you doing in my room?

711
00:40:54,535 --> 00:40:55,620
Help! Rape! Mom!

712
00:40:55,703 --> 00:40:56,704
Okay. Okay.

713
00:40:56,788 --> 00:40:58,289
Dad, help!

714
00:40:58,373 --> 00:40:59,540
Freeze, sucker!

715
00:40:59,666 --> 00:41:01,209
He jumped me!
Hey, it's okay.
I don't want any trouble.

716
00:41:01,292 --> 00:41:02,293
He came through
the window!

717
00:41:02,377 --> 00:41:04,212
You got trouble now,
you piece of trash.

718
00:41:04,879 --> 00:41:06,214
What are you doing in here
with my daughter?

719
00:41:06,297 --> 00:41:07,674
Hey, listen, I'm just
in the wrong house.

720
00:41:07,757 --> 00:41:10,218
You got that right,
you little son of a...

721
00:41:10,718 --> 00:41:11,928
Hey, look,
I made a mistake.

722
00:41:12,011 --> 00:41:14,722
Damn right you
made a mistake!

723
00:41:14,806 --> 00:41:15,807
BOY: Kill him!

724
00:41:15,890 --> 00:41:17,725
I'm gonna tear
your ass up!

725
00:41:17,809 --> 00:41:19,936
That's right.
You keep running, sucker!

726
00:41:20,019 --> 00:41:21,312
And you tell
that realty company

727
00:41:21,396 --> 00:41:22,939
that I ain't selling!
You hear?

728
00:41:23,022 --> 00:41:24,983
We ain't gonna
be terrorized!

729
00:41:35,284 --> 00:41:36,828
(DOGS HOWLING)

730
00:41:57,098 --> 00:41:59,559
(SIRENS WAILING)

731
00:42:07,108 --> 00:42:09,527
This has gotta
be the wrong year.

732
00:42:22,123 --> 00:42:23,958
(HELICOPTER WHIRRING)

733
00:42:27,628 --> 00:42:29,005
1985?

734
00:42:30,548 --> 00:42:31,591
It can't be.

735
00:42:31,674 --> 00:42:32,675
(GUN COCKING)
STRICKLAND: Drop it.

736
00:42:36,471 --> 00:42:39,223
So you're the son of
a bitch who's been
stealing my newspapers.

737
00:42:39,307 --> 00:42:40,683
Mr. Strickland.

738
00:42:41,225 --> 00:42:43,686
Mr. Strickland.
It's me, sir.
It's Marty.

739
00:42:43,770 --> 00:42:44,771
Who?

740
00:42:44,854 --> 00:42:46,064
It's Marty McFly.
Marty McFly.

741
00:42:46,147 --> 00:42:47,732
Don't you know me, sir?
From school, sir.

742
00:42:47,815 --> 00:42:49,067
I've never seen you
before in my life,

743
00:42:49,150 --> 00:42:50,860
but you look to
me like a slacker.

744
00:42:50,943 --> 00:42:53,237
Yeah, that's right.
That's right.
I am a slacker.

745
00:42:53,321 --> 00:42:55,406
Don't you remember?
You gave me detention
last week.

746
00:42:55,490 --> 00:42:58,242
Last week?
The school burned
down six years ago.

747
00:42:58,326 --> 00:42:59,410
Now, you got
exactly three seconds

748
00:42:59,494 --> 00:43:01,079
to get off my porch
with your nuts intact.

749
00:43:01,162 --> 00:43:03,081
One.
Please, Mr. Strickland,

750
00:43:03,164 --> 00:43:04,624
I just want to know
what the hell's
going on here.

751
00:43:04,707 --> 00:43:05,708
Two.
(SCREAMING)

752
00:43:05,792 --> 00:43:06,793
(TIRES SCREECHING)

753
00:43:06,876 --> 00:43:07,919
Hey, Strickland!

754
00:43:08,002 --> 00:43:09,003
BOY: Yeah!

755
00:43:18,805 --> 00:43:20,181
(EXCLAIMING)

756
00:43:26,604 --> 00:43:28,356
Eat lead, slackers!

757
00:43:28,606 --> 00:43:29,982
(SCREAMING)

758
00:43:30,066 --> 00:43:31,567
(ROCK MUSIC PLAYING)

759
00:43:35,446 --> 00:43:39,408
Watch where you're going,
crazy drunk pedestrian.

760
00:43:39,867 --> 00:43:41,202
Red.

761
00:44:37,550 --> 00:44:39,093
MAN: <i>Ladies and gentlemen,</i>

762
00:44:39,177 --> 00:44:41,679
<i>welcome to
the Biff Tannen Museum.</i>

763
00:44:41,762 --> 00:44:44,348
<i>Dedicated to Hill Valley's
number one citizen</i>

764
00:44:44,432 --> 00:44:46,851
<i>and America's
greatest living folk hero,</i>

765
00:44:46,934 --> 00:44:49,604
<i>the one and only
Biff Tannen.</i>

766
00:44:50,104 --> 00:44:53,774
<i>Of course,
we've all heard the legend,
but who is the man?</i>

767
00:44:53,858 --> 00:44:55,109
<i>Inside, you will learn</i>

768
00:44:55,193 --> 00:44:56,819
<i>how Biff Tannen became
one of the richest</i>

769
00:44:56,903 --> 00:44:59,197
<i>and most powerful men
in America.</i>

770
00:44:59,280 --> 00:45:01,949
<i>Learn the amazing history
of the Tannen family,</i>

771
00:45:02,033 --> 00:45:05,411
<i>starting with his
great-grandfather,
Buford "Mad Dog" Tannen,</i>

772
00:45:05,494 --> 00:45:07,205
<i>fastest gun in the West.</i>

773
00:45:07,288 --> 00:45:08,789
<i>See Biff's
humble beginnings</i>

774
00:45:08,873 --> 00:45:10,458
<i>and how a trip
to the racetrack</i>

775
00:45:10,541 --> 00:45:14,420
<i>on his 21st birthday
made him a millionaire
overnight.</i>

776
00:45:15,379 --> 00:45:17,590
